---
mdv: "1.0"
title: Small multiples
---

# One question asked four times

A facet is not four charts. It is one chart cut into panels over the values of
a field, and the panels share their scales — which is the whole reason the
form works, because a shared scale is what makes two panels comparable at a
glance rather than four separate y-axes that happen to sit side by side.

`column: region` cuts by region, and `columns: 2` wraps the four panels into a
two-by-two grid instead of one long row. Panel order follows first appearance
in the data, the same rule series identity uses, so filtering the data cannot
reshuffle the grid underneath a reader who has learnt where to look.

```mdv line
title: Signups by region
x: week
y: signups
column: region
columns: 2
---
region,week,signups
north,2026-06-01,120
north,2026-06-08,138
north,2026-06-15,151
south,2026-06-01,86
south,2026-06-08,92
south,2026-06-15,79
east,2026-06-01,204
east,2026-06-08,188
east,2026-06-15,231
west,2026-06-01,41
west,2026-06-08,55
west,2026-06-15,62
```

Binding both `row:` and `column:` asks for the grid the two fields describe,
so the wrap has nothing left to decide and is ignored. Axes are drawn on the
outer panels only: the left column carries the value axis and the bottom row
the category axis, because repeating an identical ladder in every cell is ink
that is not data.

```mdv bar
title: Signups by channel and quarter
x: channel
y: signups
row: channel
column: quarter
---
channel,quarter,signups
organic,Q1,310
organic,Q2,348
paid,Q1,190
paid,Q2,142
```
